## Why This Is Asked
Practical beginner task validating end-to-end ingestion, data quality, and cataloging.

## Key Concepts
- S3 event-driven ETL
- Glue Spark/DynamicFrame validation
- Quarantine routing for bad records
- Parquet partitioning by date
- Glue Data Catalog and Athena access

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo Glue-like JS snippet for validation
function process(batch){
  const valid=[], quarantined=[];
  for (const r of batch){
    if (r.a != null && r.b != null) valid.push(transform(r));
    else quarantined.push({row:r, validation_reason:'missing_required_fields'});
  }
  writeParquet(valid, datePartition);
  writeJson(quarantined, quarantineBucket);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test schema evolution if new fields are added?
- How would you monitor the quarantine rate and alert if it spikes?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design real-time data quality and drift detection at scale, balancing schema evolution, validation, quarantine, alerting, and cross-account governance using AWS services.

## Key Concepts
- Schema evolution and registry management
- Real-time validation and data quarantining
- Observability: CloudWatch metrics and SNS alerts
- Cross-account auditability via Glue Data Catalog and Lake Formation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo validator for a streaming record
function isRecordValid(r){
  if(!r.user_id || !r.timestamp) return false;
  if(typeof r.value !== 'number' || r.value < 0) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test drift detection across regions and accounts?
- What are trade-offs of using a separate quarantine bucket vs inline rejection for analytics workloads?
